### Q3: What is Definition of Done?
**A**: Explicit agreement on quality criteria that must be met before considering work complete. Prevents "80% done" syndrome.

**Practical Example - Real Team's Definition of Done:**

```
✓ Code is written following team's style guide (eslint config)
✓ Peer code review completed and approved by ≥1 reviewer
✓ Unit tests written with ≥80% code coverage
✓ Tests pass locally and in CI/CD pipeline
✓ Integration tests pass for related modules
✓ Feature tested in QA environment against acceptance criteria
✓ No console errors or warnings
✓ Performance acceptable (page load <2s, API response <500ms)
✓ Accessibility requirements met (WCAG 2.1 AA standard)
✓ API documentation updated (Swagger/OpenAPI)
✓ Product Owner has reviewed and accepted
✓ Code merged to main branch
✓ Feature deployed to staging environment
✓ No regression in other features
```

**Story Example - NOT Done vs DONE:**

```
❌ NOT DONE (Common mistake):
- Code written and merged
- "Works on my machine"
- No tests
- Documentation incomplete
- PO hasn't seen it yet

✅ DONE (Properly):
- Code merged and tested
- All acceptance criteria met
- Tests passing (80%+ coverage)
- Documentation updated
- PO reviewed and accepted
- Deployed to staging
- Ready for production
```

**Interview Context:** Shows understanding of quality standards, prevents shortcuts, ensures consistency. Demonstrates how successful teams maintain quality while moving fast.

