### Interview Scenario 3: Distributed Team Challenges

**Scenario:** Team across 3 time zones, coordination difficult

**Traditional Problem:**
```
├─ Standups: Can't all make it live
├─ Communication: Async confuses people
├─ Blockers: Wait until next sync
├─ Collaboration: Delayed, frustrating
└─ Result: Team feels disconnected
```

**Agile Solution:**
```
Communication Strategy:

Synchronous (Live):
├─ Standup: 9 AM PST (9, 12, 4 in zones)
│  └─ Or async updates in Slack if can't attend
├─ Planning: Weekend afternoon (works for all)
├─ Retro: 30 min, recorded for async team
└─ Design review: 1 hour weekly

Asynchronous:
├─ Slack updates: "I completed X, starting Y, blocked by Z"
├─ Code review: Within 4 hours (not blocking overnight)
├─ Refinement: Document, comment, async estimation
├─ Decisions: Recorded, shared, async feedback 24 hours
└─ Documentation: Written (not meetings)

Tools:
├─ Slack: Daily communication
├─ Jira: Visible work status
├─ GitHub: Code review process
├─ Confluence: Documentation
├─ Zoom: Recorded meetings for async
└─ Miro: Whiteboarding for retros

Example Flow:

Day 1 (Zone A morning):
├─ Dev A: Completes feature, creates PR
├─ PR: Visible in GitHub, Slack notification
└─ Dev A: Blocks on code review (Zone B/C not up yet)

Day 1 (Zone B morning):
├─ Dev B: Reviews code, leaves feedback
├─ Dev A (now online): Addresses feedback
└─ Dev B: Approves

Day 1 (Zone C morning):
├─ Dev C: Merges code
├─ Everyone: Sees status updated
└─ No one blocked!

Standup (async example):
Dev A (9 AM PST): "Yesterday: Completed login form.
Today: Testing. No blockers."

Dev B (noon PST): "Yesterday: API integration.
Today: Performance testing. BLOCKED: Need Dev A's
API contract" (Dev A: Reply posted 2 hours later)

Dev C (4 PM PST): "Yesterday: Database migration.
Today: Optimization. No blockers."

SM (reads all): "Sees blocker, follows up with A.
Blocker resolved same day because of clear communication."
```

**Interview Answer:**
"With distributed teams, async-first is critical. Standups can
be live with overlap, but communication needs to be written and
visible. I use Slack for daily sync, GitHub for code visibility,
and Jira for task tracking. The key is not waiting for sync
meetings - decisions documented, feedback given within hours,
blockers clear. I worked with teams across PST/EST/GMT and
we moved faster than co-located teams because communication
was explicit and asynchronous."

