## Comprehensive Interview Questions & Answers

### JUNIOR LEVEL QUESTIONS

#### Q1: What is Scrum?
**A:** Scrum is an Agile framework for managing projects through iterative development.

**Complete Answer for Interview:**
"Scrum is an Agile framework built on three pillars: transparency, inspection, and adaptation. It provides a structure for teams to work in time-boxed iterations called sprints, typically 1-4 weeks.

Key components:
- **Roles**: Product Owner (prioritizes work), Scrum Master (removes blockers), Development Team (delivers)
- **Artifacts**: Product Backlog (what to build), Sprint Backlog (what's building this sprint), Increment (working product)
- **Ceremonies**: Sprint Planning, Daily Standup, Sprint Review, Sprint Retrospective

Why it works:
- Regular feedback loops prevent building the wrong thing
- Short iterations allow course correction
- Team empowerment improves quality and motivation
- Metrics (velocity) enable realistic planning

I've used Scrum for 2 years on payment systems. Predictable delivery and team happiness improved significantly."

---

#### Q2: Explain the three roles in Scrum
**A:** Product Owner, Scrum Master, Development Team

**Complete Answer:**
```
Product Owner:
├─ Owns product vision and roadmap
├─ Manages and prioritizes Product Backlog
├─ Accepts/rejects completed work
├─ Represents customers and stakeholders
├─ Accountable for ROI
└─ Makes business decisions

Example: "At my last company, PO met with customers weekly,
gathered feedback, and reprioritized the backlog based on
new insights. This led to 40% increase in feature adoption."

Scrum Master:
├─ Facilitates Scrum ceremonies
├─ Removes impediments (blockers)
├─ Coaches team on Scrum practices
├─ Protects team from interruptions
├─ Servant leader (not manager)
└─ No authority over developers

Example: "When the payment API wasn't ready mid-sprint,
I escalated to the API team lead, got them prioritized,
and unblocked the team same day."

Development Team:
├─ Self-organizing (no task assignment)
├─ Cross-functional (all skills present)
├─ Accountable for delivery
├─ Estimates work
├─ Owns code quality and Definition of Done
├─ Typically 5-9 people
└─ Commits to sprint goals collaboratively

Example: "My team of 6 was backend-heavy, so we paired
a senior backend dev with a junior frontend dev. This
improved knowledge sharing and code quality."
```

---

#### Q3: What is a Sprint and how long should it be?
**A:** A Sprint is a fixed time-box (usually 2 weeks) where team commits to completing specific work.

**Complete Answer:**
```
Sprint Definition:
├─ Fixed duration (typically 1-4 weeks, most common: 2 weeks)
├─ Consistent time-box (same length each sprint)
├─ Team commits to sprint goal
├─ Work doesn't change mid-sprint
├─ Ends with review and retrospective
└─ Followed immediately by next sprint

Typical Sprint Duration:
1-week sprints:
├─ Pros: Faster feedback, more frequent releases
├─ Cons: Less time to build features, overhead high
└─ Best for: Support teams, operations

2-week sprints (most common):
├─ Pros: Good balance of complexity and ceremony overhead
├─ Cons: Still relatively frequent ceremonies
└─ Best for: Development teams building features

4-week sprints (month-long):
├─ Pros: Time for complex features
├─ Cons: Slow feedback, harder to predict
└─ Best for: Large, complex features (rare)

Why consistent length matters:
├─ Velocity becomes reliable (predictable capacity)
├─ Team develops rhythm
├─ Planning is standardized
└─ Easier to forecast

Example: "My team switched from inconsistent sprint lengths
to consistent 2-week sprints. Velocity stabilized from
10-25 range to 18-22. Predictability improved 90%."
```

---

#### Q4: What is a User Story?
**A:** A simple description of a feature from end-user perspective with acceptance criteria.

**Complete Answer:**
```
User Story Format:
As a [user type],
I want [specific action],
So that [benefit/value]

Example:
As a customer,
I want to save my payment method,
So that I don't have to enter it every time I checkout

Key characteristics:
✓ Simple and understandable
✓ Independent (can work on alone)
✓ Negotiable (details can be discussed)
✓ Valuable (delivers customer benefit)
✓ Estimable (team can estimate)
✓ Small (1-5 days of work)
✓ Testable (acceptance criteria defined)

Acceptance Criteria (How to verify it's done):
✓ User can click "Save Payment Method" checkbox
✓ Payment method stored securely
✓ User can select saved method on next checkout
✓ User can delete a saved method
✓ Works on mobile and desktop

Definition of Done (Quality standard):
✓ Code peer reviewed
✓ Unit tests pass (>80% coverage)
✓ QA testing passed
✓ Performance acceptable
✓ No security issues
✓ PO accepted

Why this matters:
- Creates shared understanding between dev and PO
- Prevents "I thought it meant..."
- Enables testing and verification
- Smaller scope = easier to estimate

My experience: Stories without acceptance criteria
led to rework. Once we enforced good user stories,
our sprint velocity became predictable and quality improved."
```

---

#### Q5: What is Definition of Done?
**A:** Explicit agreement on quality criteria that must be met before work is considered complete.

**Complete Answer:**
```
Definition of Done (DoD) is the team's quality standard.

Without DoD:
├─ Ambiguous completion (what's "done"?)
├─ Quality inconsistent
├─ Rework in later sprints
├─ Production bugs increase
└─ Team frustrated

With DoD:
├─ Clear exit criteria
├─ Consistent quality
├─ Less rework
├─ Fewer production issues
└─ Team aligned

Typical DoD Includes:
Code Quality:
├─ Code written and formatted
├─ Peer reviewed (≥1 reviewer)
├─ No linting warnings
└─ Follows team standards

Testing:
├─ Unit tests (>80% coverage)
├─ Integration tests pass
├─ Acceptance criteria verified
└─ QA testing complete

Deployment:
├─ Deployed to staging
├─ No deployment errors
├─ Health checks passing
└─ Ready for production

Business:
├─ Product Owner acceptance
├─ Documentation updated
├─ Stakeholder approval
└─ No tech debt introduced (or tracked)

Real Example - Payment Feature:
Story: User Can Pay with Credit Card

NOT DONE (Mistake):
├─ Code written
├─ Compiles
├─ "Looks good to me"
└─ → Ships to production, crashes!

DONE (Correct):
├─ Code written & reviewed
├─ Unit tests (85% coverage)
├─ Integration tests pass
├─ Manual QA testing complete
├─ Security review passed
├─ Deployed to staging
├─ PO acceptance verified
└─ → Confident in production release

DoD prevents "80% done" syndrome where work sits unfinished."
```

