3. Why not caught earlier? "Requirements unclear"
4. Why unclear? "We didn't refine properly"
5. Why not refined? "Skipped refinement to "save time""

Reaction:
"This was frustrating initially. But I realized this was a
learning opportunity, not a failure."

What We Did About It:
├─ In retro, team discussed root causes
├─ We identified: Refinement is investment, not overhead
├─ Action: Committed to 1.5hr weekly refinement
├─ Adjusted sprint: Estimated more conservatively

Results:
├─ Sprint 4: 20 points committed, 19 delivered
├─ Sprint 5: 21 points committed, 20 delivered
├─ Velocity stabilized
├─ Team confidence increased
└─ Sprint goals consistently achieved

Lesson Learned:
"Trying to skip refinement to 'save time' cost us time.
Investing in process pays dividends. Also learned that
sprints sometimes fail - that's ok if you learn from it."

What I'd Do Differently:
"Now I'd recommend conservative commitment in sprint 1,
establish good refinement, then improve over time.
Build sustainable pace rather than trying to be heroes."
```

Why This Answer Works:
✓ Honest (admits mistake, doesn't blame)
✓ Specific (actual example with numbers)
✓ Root cause analysis (not just symptoms)
✓ Action taken (proved learning)
✓ Results shown (velocity improved)
✓ Self-reflection (lessons for future)
```

