#### Q11: How do you handle a team that's constantly behind schedule?
**A**: Diagnose root causes, implement sustainable improvements.

**Complete Answer:**
```
Scenario:
Team committed 20 points, consistently delivering 12-15

Investigation Process:

Step 1 - COLLECT DATA:
Metrics to analyze:
├─ Historical velocity (last 8 sprints)
├─ Story point breakdown by size
├─ Burndown charts (how work progresses)
├─ Time tracking (where hours go)
├─ Defect rates (bugs causing delays)
└─ Deployment frequency

Step 2 - INTERVIEW TEAM:
Ask questions:
├─ "What blocks you most often?"
├─ "Do you feel the estimates are realistic?"
├─ "What would help you deliver more?"
├─ "Are stories clear when you start?"
└─ "Do you have dependencies on other teams?"

Step 3 - IDENTIFY ROOT CAUSES:

Possible Cause #1: Over-Commitment
├─ Team estimates 20 points
├─ But true capacity is 12 points
├─ Solution: Reduce commitment, be realistic
├─ Result: 100% delivery, team happy

Possible Cause #2: Unclear Requirements
├─ Stories unclear when development starts
├─ Rework happens mid-sprint
├─ Solution: Better refinement, clear AC
├─ Result: Less rework, faster delivery

Possible Cause #3: Technical Debt
├─ Codebase hard to work with
├─ Takes 2x longer than should
├─ Solution: Allocate 20-30% to tech debt
├─ Result: Over 2-3 sprints, velocity improves

Possible Cause #4: Blocking Dependencies
├─ Waiting on other teams
├─ Lost productivity time
├─ Solution: Parallel work, mock dependencies
├─ Result: Teams don't block each other

Possible Cause #5: Interruptions
├─ Support tickets, bugs, urgent requests
├─ Derail sprint work
├─ Solution: Dedicated support person OR
│  Reserve 20% capacity for interruptions
├─ Result: Rest of team focused

Possible Cause #6: Skill Gaps
├─ Complex tasks take longer
├─ Junior devs struggling
├─ Solution: Pair programming, training
├─ Result: Skills improve, delivery increases

Possible Cause #7: Quality Debt
├─ Insufficient testing in sprint
├─ Bugs found in QA or production
├─ Rework next sprint
├─ Solution: Test during sprint, not after
├─ Result: Less rework, true delivery

Most Common Root Causes (In Order):
1. Over-commitment (35% of cases)
2. Unclear requirements (30%)
3. Technical debt (20%)
4. Blocking dependencies (10%)
5. Other (5%)

Real Example from My Experience:
```
TEAM: E-commerce Platform (6 developers)
Problem: Consistent miss (commit 22, deliver 14)
Duration: 4 sprints

Investigation Results:
├─ Interview: "We don't understand requirements"
├─ Data: 40% of time rework on unclear stories
├─ Burndown: Stayed flat first week, sprint rush end
├─ Tech debt: Moderate (not major factor)
└─ Dependencies: Few (mostly self-contained)

Diagnosis: Requirement clarity

Solution Implemented:
├─ Added structured refinement (1.5 hr/week)
├─ PO explains context, not just requirements
├─ Team asks clarifying questions in refinement
├─ Acceptance criteria written clearly
├─ Examples included in stories

Results:
├─ Sprint 5: Commit 22, Deliver 20 (91%)
├─ Sprint 6: Commit 21, Deliver 21 (100%)
├─ Sprint 7+: Consistent 20-22 delivery
├─ Team morale: Improved (no longer failing)
├─ Quality: Improved (better requirements = fewer bugs)
```

Implementation Plan:

Week 1-2: Root Cause Analysis
├─ Data collection
├─ Team interviews
├─ Identify top 2-3 issues
└─ Present findings to team

Week 3-4: Experiments
├─ Implement improvements
├─ Try multiple approaches
├─ Measure impact
└─ Team feedback

Week 5-8: Measurement
├─ Track velocity
├─ Monitor metrics
├─ Adjust based on results
└─ Celebrate improvements

Key Principles:
✓ Diagnose, don't assume
✓ Data-driven (not emotional)
✓ Involve team in solutions
✓ Sustainable pace over heroics
✓ Celebrate improvements
```

